# {{PROJECT_NAME}} - Cursor Development Rules

This file defines project-specific rules for Cursor.

## Critical Rules

### Issue / Branch Creation (Auto-Execute Required)

**AI must auto-execute at work start (no user confirmation needed):**

1. **Create Issue first** - `gh issue create` to get Issue number
2. **Create branch** - `git checkout -b [type]/[description]-[issue-number]`
3. **No work on main** - Direct work on main is prohibited
4. **Include in PR** - `closes #[issue-number]`
5. **Server ports are fixed** - Stop existing processes, use only configured ports, and stop servers after work

### Autonomy Principles

AI should act autonomously by default.

Ask the user only when:

1. The answer cannot be determined after investigation
2. An unexpected situation causes a loop or prevents reliable progress
3. Design is complete and implementation approval is required

Do not ask questions that can be answered by reading code, docs, command output, or existing project configuration.

When errors occur, investigate and fix them autonomously if a durable fix is clear. Do not use temporary workarounds such as disabling tests, swallowing errors, weakening types, or bypassing quality gates. If a durable fix is unclear, stop and ask.

### Subagent Model Selection

<!-- Note: unlike CLAUDE.md, this file intentionally has no "Plan Execution Rules"
     section. Cursor has no Task-tool equivalent for subagent-driven plan execution,
     so execution method is not specified here. Do not "fix" this asymmetry when
     syncing templates. -->

Cursor can choose the model per subagent invocation. **When dispatching subagents, switch the model according to the nature of the request instead of always using the same one:**

| Task Type | Model Choice | Examples |
|-----------|--------------|----------|
| Initial planning / design framing | Fable if available, otherwise Claude Opus 5 or GPT-5.6-Sol | First-pass requirements decomposition, architecture option framing, implementation strategy design |
| Codebase exploration / context gathering | Claude Sonnet 5 (or a comparable fast model, excluding Claude Haiku) | Understanding project structure in Phase 1, finding related implementations, tracing dependencies |
| Simple mechanical search | Claude Sonnet 5 (or a comparable fast model, excluding Claude Haiku) | File/symbol lookup, listing usages |
| Implementation / concrete work | Claude Opus 5, Composer 2.5 Fast, GPT-5.6-Terra, or GPT-5.6-Sol with lowered effort | Phase 2/3 implementation, test writing, refactoring |
| Document review (spec / plan documents) | Claude Sonnet 5 (or a comparable fast model, excluding Claude Haiku) | Spec-document and plan-document reviewers during design/planning |
| Quality / architecture review, quality-check personas | Claude Opus 5 first; GPT-5.6-Sol high as cross-vendor alternative | 6-persona quality-check review (Phase 5), code review, security analysis, architecture critique |

This table takes precedence over any model-selection guidance inside individual skills (e.g. the "Model Selection" section of subagent-driven-development). When in doubt between two tiers, choose the more capable model, but keep Fable reserved for the initial planning/design pass. If Fable is unavailable, use Claude Opus 5 or GPT-5.6-Sol for planning/design. Implementation work should NOT run on Fable - use Claude Opus 5, Composer 2.5 Fast, GPT-5.6-Terra, or a lowered-effort GPT-5.6-Sol. Do not use Claude Haiku for any tier. Claude Sonnet is allowed only for exploration/search tasks and document reviews (spec/plan documents) - never use it for implementation, quality-check personas, or planning. Per-task reviewers are not dispatched at all (Review Consolidation rule 1). For quality, architecture and security reviews and the quality-check personas, prefer Claude Opus 5, with GPT-5.6-Sol high when cross-vendor diversity is useful; never downgrade those to Sonnet. Do not use Fable for review agents.

### Review Consolidation

Review loops exist at exactly two points (quality policy §5.5): **document creation** (spec / plan documents) and the **final quality-check gate** (Phase 5). **Phase 5 is never relaxed**: it is the final `quality-check` gate itself, where quality policy §5 and the completion criteria in the `quality-check` skill apply (every High/Medium consolidated finding addressed; the skill holds the full definition - read it there).

1. **No in-development reviews** - While implementing (Phases 2-4), do NOT run per-task or per-implementation review rounds with review agents. **This overrides both review stages in `.cursor/skills/superpowers/subagent-driven-development/SKILL.md`: the per-task two-stage review loop and the final whole-branch code review at plan end** (as the model table above overrides that skill's Model Selection section) - the whole-branch review covers the same full diff that Phase 5's quality-check already reviews, so running it would be a duplicate. The implementer's self-review (run the tests, verify the diff against the task/spec text) is the only in-development check. Quality assurance happens once, at Phase 5.
2. **Document reviews stay** - The spec-document and plan-document review loops (during design/planning) are retained: run a document reviewer, with the iteration cap and escalation rule defined in `documents/development/quality-policy.md` §5.5 (read them there - do not restate the numbers here). **This overrides the "fix inline, no need to re-review" guidance in the brainstorming / writing-plans skills.** A wrong design or plan compounds through every task and cannot be repaired by the final gate.
3. **Budget escalation** - State an expected agent-invocation count up front and escalate to the user when the work is projected to exceed it.
4. **Do not reinstate per-task reviews** - If Phase 5 produces many findings, route the causes instead: mechanically detectable issues to the lint layer, design-level issues into the spec/plan review checklists.
5. **Platform constraints are confirmed first** - Designs or verifications that rely on a platform constraint (rate limits, execution time caps, size limits) MUST confirm the constraint against official documentation before work starts - never assume a limit from memory.

### Shell Command Rules (Windows / PowerShell)

When the execution shell is PowerShell (default on Windows), bash idioms fail. Follow these rules:

1. **No `&&` / `||` chaining** - Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not support them. Use `;` for unconditional sequencing, and check `$LASTEXITCODE` for conditional execution
2. **No bash heredocs** (`<<EOF`) - use PowerShell here-strings (`$body = @'...'@`) instead
3. **No bash test syntax** (`[ -f file ]`) - use `Test-Path` / `if (...)`
4. **No `/dev/null`** - redirect with `2>$null` or pipe to `Out-Null`
5. **Quote paths containing spaces** and prefer `Join-Path` for path construction
6. **Complex one-liners break easily** (quoting, `?:` operator is PowerShell 7+ only) - write a temporary script file and execute it instead

## Phase Design Workflow

### Phase 1: Requirements Analysis
- Understand project structure
- Check existing implementation
- Review related API specs
- Check `.cursor/rules/` rule files
- Judge the risk level of the change (quality policy §1). For High/Medium risk, run `test-design` before implementation to define test oracles and falsification items (quality policy §4)

### Phase 2: Backend Implementation
- Use centralized exception handling
- No full package names (use import statements)

### Phase 3: Frontend Implementation
- Reference Phase 1, 2 results

### Phase 4: Self-Review (no reviewer agents)
- Implementer self-review only: run the tests, verify the diff against the spec/plan text (design consistency, documentation consistency, DRY)
- Architecture-level review is covered by the Phase 5 quality-check personas - do not run a separate review round here (Review Consolidation rule 1)

### Phase 5: Quality Verification
- Determine the change area **and risk level** first (quality policy §1); `quality-check` records it as `risk_level`
- Run backend quality checks
- Run frontend linting, formatting, build (after running the `lint-scaffolding` skill, `lint:all` is the registered static check command and `quality-check` Step 2 runs it as the deterministic check layer)
- Run multi-persona review via `quality-check`, which owns a single outer cycle (static checks -> tests -> personas -> consolidated-findings fixes; staged personas by diff size: <200 lines -> 3 personas, 200+ -> 6; min 1 cycle, run another cycle only on High/Medium consolidated findings / docs+infra only: reduced personas). Cycles 2+ are a verification review: only the personas that raised High/Medium findings in the previous cycle re-review the fix diffs and responses; a new high finding reverts the next cycle to the full persona set over the full diff. The QA persona works falsification-first: hunt for inputs that prove the implementation wrong, rather than confirming the tests look sufficient
- After the cycle completes, run the additional-test proposal (test-recommendation skill: heuristics recommend mutation/E2E runs with rationale, the user decides, approved runs execute; declines are recorded and never block - except an executed E2E that fails must be fixed)
- Run `self-improvement` before merge completion (the gate applies to merges into main, not feature-branch pushes)
- Harness-only diffs (`CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.cursorrules`, `.claude/**`, `.codex/**`, `.cursor/**`, `skills/project/**`, `skills/superpowers/**`, `.github/review-*.md`, `documents/development/coding-rules/**`) skip the gate and the quality check entirely. **Carve-out**: a diff that changes gate parameters - the `Quality Gate Overrides` keys below - is NOT exempt and must go through quality-check (a reduced-persona review at minimum). The same applies to gate control-plane files - `skills/project/quality-check/**`, `skills/project/_schemas/**` and `skills/project/test-recommendation/**`, the `.claude/skills` and `.claude/hooks` trees (including the `.claude/skills` symlink node itself - `init` creates it as a link, and re-pointing that one path swaps the whole quality-check tree) and their `.codex/**` / `.cursor/**` copies, `.github/review-*.md`, the hook registration files (`.codex/hooks.json` and the `.claude` / `.cursor` equivalents), `.codex/config.toml` (whole-file: it can carry an inline `[hooks]` table, a `[features]` hooks kill-switch and `[[rules]]` deny decisions), the MCP registration files (`.claude/mcp.json` / `.codex/mcp.json` / `.cursor/mcp.json` - MCP server definitions register command execution, the same class as hooks), and the hook registration inside `.claude/settings.json` AND `.claude/settings.local.json` (Claude Code reads both, the latter at higher precedence): the `hooks` block, the `disableAllHooks` / `allowManagedHooksOnly` hook-disable keys, and the `permissions.deny` rule list (the deny layer backing the destructive-command guards; `permissions.allow` changes stay exempt) - unregistering or disabling the hook, or weakening the deny layer, disables protection just as surely as editing the hook does. Path matching is case-insensitive (`.claude/Hooks/...` is the same file as `.claude/hooks/...` on Windows/macOS). With in-development reviews consolidated into Phase 5, these must never change with zero review (the quality-gate hook enforces this carve-out - it blocks such diffs with `Gate control-plane changed:`, and a post-check commit touching these files invalidates `.quality-check-passed`). If main advanced independently after the flag was issued, the merged main (feature + main combined) is unverified: the merge may succeed but the push to main is blocked - re-run `quality-check` on the merged main, then push
- Thresholds, cycle caps and budgets live in `documents/development/quality-policy.md` - read them there instead of restating numbers here

